<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-matrix-algebra" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Matrix Algebra</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A matrix is more than a bookkeeping device for a system of linear equations:
      matrices can be added, scaled, multiplied, and <mdash /> sometimes <mdash />
      inverted. In this chapter we develop that arithmetic, paying close attention to
      which familiar rules of algebra survive the move from numbers to matrices and
      which do not. The chapter closes with applications, where the operations developed
      here are put to work on a problem from outside mathematics.
    </p>
  </introduction>

